    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d233">

                <pb facs="0461" n="387" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg387"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the President</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-01">January 1,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I told the Secretary of State I thought it inadvisable to resuscitate this
                    question by making a statement;<anchor n="1" xml:id="d233-f1"/> that the French
                    Admiral has already declined to leave the St. Pierre-Miquelon; that we cannot
                    afford to send an expedition to bomb him out and that Sumner Welles could best
                    handle this situation verbally when he gets to Rio on the above basis.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d233-f2"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>F. D. R.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d233-f1" n="1" xml:id="d233-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably the draft statement <hi rendition="#italic">supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f2" n="2" xml:id="d233-f2-content">
                <p>There is no evidence that this question came up at the Conference of American
                    Foreign Ministers held at Rio de Janeiro, January 15–28, 1942. For documentation
                    on that Conference, see <ref target="frus1942v05#pg_6">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1942, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, pp. 6</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
